(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-anatomy-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What is circumduction?","choices":["A purely rotational movement occurring solely about a single vertical axis","A movement unique to the eye, termed intorsion and extorsion","Movement of the thumb into an opposed position against the fingers only","A combined movement involving flexion/extension plus abduction/adduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Circumduction is a combined movement involving two pairs of movement: flexion/extension plus abduction/adduction. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is opposition/reposition of the thumb described as in the material?","choices":["A specific movement that is described as occurring only at the wrist joint","A movement mechanically identical to circumduction of the joint","A unique human characteristic involving rotation about a resultant axis","A purely rotational movement of the eyeball within its socket only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Opposition/reposition of the thumb is described as a unique human characteristic, involving rotation about a resultant axis. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What are the two sets of internal body cavities described in the material?","choices":["Thoracic cavity and pelvic cavity","Dorsal body cavity and ventral body cavity","Cranial cavity and abdominal cavity","The synovial cavity together with the oral cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two sets of internal body cavities are the dorsal body cavity and the ventral body cavity, both closed to the environment and providing differing degrees of protection to organs. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the dorsal body cavity protect, and what are its two subdivisions?","choices":["Protects the digestive organs; its subdivisions are the thoracic cavity and the pelvic cavity","Protects the nervous system; subdivided into the cranial cavity (encasing the brain) and vertebral cavity (encasing the spinal cord)","Protects the reproductive organs; its subdivisions are the paired left and right pleural cavities","Protects the digestive organs; its subdivisions are the thoracic cavity and the abdominal cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dorsal body cavity protects the nervous system and has two subdivisions: the cranial cavity, which encases the brain, and the vertebral cavity, which encases the spinal cord. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the ventral body cavity house, and what separates its two subdivisions?","choices":["Houses internal organs (viscera); the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities are separated by the diaphragm","Houses reproductive organs exclusively; its subdivisions are separated by the muscular pelvic floor","Houses only digestive organs; subdivisions separated by the mesentery","Houses the brain and the spinal cord; its subdivisions are separated by the bony vertebral column"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ventral body cavity houses internal organs (viscera) and has two subdivisions — the thoracic cavity and the abdominopelvic cavity — separated by the diaphragm. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the subdivisions of the thoracic cavity, and what does the mediastinum contain?","choices":["Only the pericardial and paired pleural cavities, with no mediastinum described at all","Two pleural cavities (each housing a lung) and the mediastinum, which contains the pericardial cavity, esophagus, trachea, and thymus","The abdominal and pelvic cavities, containing the stomach, intestines, and bladder","Two separate pericardial cavities, one enclosing each lung individually rather than the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thoracic cavity's subdivisions are two pleural cavities (each housing a lung) and the mediastinum, which contains the pericardial cavity, esophagus, trachea, thymus, thoracic duct, and cardiac and phrenic nerves, surrounding the thoracic organs. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What are the subdivisions of the abdominopelvic cavity, and what does each contain?","choices":["Cranial cavity, containing the brain, and vertebral cavity, containing the spinal cord","Pleural cavity, containing the lungs, and pericardial cavity, containing the heart","Thoracic cavity, containing the heart and lungs, and pelvic cavity, containing the bladder","Abdominal cavity (stomach, intestines, spleen, liver) and pelvic cavity (urinary bladder, reproductive organs, rectum)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The abdominopelvic cavity's subdivisions are the abdominal cavity, containing the stomach, intestines, spleen, and liver, and the pelvic cavity, containing the urinary bladder, reproductive organs, and rectum. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What are the three named serous membranes, and with which cavity/organ is each associated?","choices":["Pericardium (heart), pleurae (lungs), and peritoneum (abdominopelvic cavity)","Pericardium (lungs), pleurae (heart), and peritoneum (brain)","Peritoneum (heart), pleurae (abdominopelvic cavity), and pericardium (lungs)","Meninges (brain), pleurae (lungs), and pericardium (heart)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three named serous membranes are the pericardium (associated with the heart), the pleurae (associated with the lungs), and the peritoneum (associated with the abdominopelvic cavity). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the difference between parietal serosa and visceral serosa?","choices":["Both layers are said to specifically cover internal organs only, never the cavity walls","Parietal serosa lines internal body cavity walls; visceral serosa covers internal organs","Both layers are said to specifically line the body cavity walls only, never the organs","Parietal serosa covers organs; visceral serosa lines cavity walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parietal serosa lines the internal body cavity walls, while visceral serosa covers the internal organs (viscera); the two layers are separated by a slit-like cavity filled with serous fluid. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the function of the serous fluid secreted by the serous membrane layers?","choices":["Filters the blood plasma of waste products","Protects and provides lubrication (antifriction)","Transports nutrients to organs","Produces new immune cells for the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"The serous fluid, secreted by both layers of the serous membrane, protects and provides lubrication (an antifriction function) as organs move against the cavity wall. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the Haversian system (osteon), and what does the Haversian canal contain?","choices":["A specialized type of cartilage located in the epiphysis; it is populated by chondrocytes","The fibrous outer covering of bone; it contains only lymphatic vessels and no blood supply","A specialized type of bone marrow; it is populated by hematopoietic stem cells","The structural unit of compact bone; the Haversian canal contains blood vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Haversian system (osteon) is a structural unit of bone histology, and its central Haversian canal contains blood vessels. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are lamellae, in bone histology?","choices":["The canals connecting osteocytes to each other","Small spaces housing osteocytes","Concentric rings of hard bone","The outer fibrous covering of bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lamellae are concentric rings of hard bone, part of the Haversian system's structure. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are canaliculi, in bone histology?","choices":["The outer fibrous covering of bone, continuous with the tendon attachments","Concentric rings of hard bone matrix surrounding the central Haversian canal","Connecting tunnels linking osteocytes in lacunae to each other and to the Haversian canal","The thin membrane lining the inner surface of the bone, facing the marrow cavity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Canaliculi are the connecting tunnels that link osteocytes in their lacunae to each other and to the Haversian canal. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the difference between the periosteum and endosteum?","choices":["Periosteum and endosteum both refer only to cartilage","Periosteum lines the internal surfaces of bone, while endosteum forms the outer covering","Both terms are said to describe the exact identical fibrous covering layer of bone tissue","Periosteum is the outer covering of bone; endosteum lines the internal bone surfaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"The periosteum is the outer covering of bone, while the endosteum lines the internal (inner) bone surfaces. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the difference between compact (cortical) bone and trabecular (cancellous) bone?","choices":["Both terms describe the identical dense outer bone structure with Haversian systems","Compact bone forms the spongy internal lattice, while trabecular bone forms the dense outer shell","Compact bone is dense outer bone with Haversian systems; trabecular bone is the internal, lattice-like spongy bone","Compact and trabecular bone are both said to refer only to types of hyaline cartilage tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Compact (cortical) bone is the dense outer bone tissue containing Haversian systems, while trabecular (cancellous) bone is the internal, lattice-like spongy bone. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where is red marrow found, and what is its function?","choices":["Contains cartilage-forming chondrocytes","Contains osteoclasts only, functioning in bone resorption","Contains hematopoietic tissue and produces blood cells","Contains only fat and functions as an energy store"],"answer":2,"exp":"Red marrow contains hematopoietic tissue and produces blood cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are the zones of the epiphyseal (growth) plate in a long bone, in order?","choices":["Only two zones exist: resting and calcification","Zone of calcification, zone of hypertrophy, zone of proliferation, zone of resting cartilage","Zone of resting cartilage, zone of proliferation, zone of hypertrophy, zone of calcification","Zone of ossification, zone of resting cartilage, zone of proliferation, zone of hypertrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"The epiphyseal plate's zones, in order from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis, are the zone of resting cartilage, zone of proliferation, zone of hypertrophy, and zone of calcification. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What happens to chondrocytes as they progress through the growth plate zones?","choices":["They divide and stack on top of one another, then die upon calcification as blood vessels from the diaphysis grow into the area","They remain entirely static within the resting zone of the growth plate and never divide throughout the process","They transform directly into osteoclasts that resorb the surrounding calcified cartilage matrix","They immediately die upon entering the resting zone of the growth plate, before any proliferation occurs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chondrocytes divide and stack on top of one another as they progress through the growth plate, then die upon calcification, at which point blood vessels from the diaphysis grow into the area. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"With what structures is muscle tissue associated, per the material?","choices":["Only the central nervous system's supporting glial structures","Only the walls of blood vessels and lymphatic channels","The bones of the skeleton, the heart, and the walls of hollow organs","Only the bones of the axial and appendicular skeleton"],"answer":2,"exp":"Muscle tissue is associated with the bones of the skeleton, the heart, and the walls of the hollow organs of the body. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of muscle tissue?","choices":["Produces heat and maintains posture","Transmits impulses and sensations","Stores minerals and lipids","Filters and reabsorbs fluids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Muscle tissue functions include movement, locomotion, maintaining posture, producing heat, facial expressions, pumping blood, and peristalsis. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is \"dual innervation,\" and which organs have it?","choices":["Only the heart and lungs have dual innervation; every other organ has single innervation","Most vital organs are innervated by both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems","Dual innervation refers specifically to innervation from two separate spinal cord segments","Dual innervation means innervation by both somatic and autonomic fibers exclusively together"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dual innervation means most vital organs are innervated by both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is the sympathetic division most active, and what are its general effects?","choices":["Most active during rest and sleep; causes relaxation, promotes digestion, and gradual, ongoing energy storage processes throughout","Most active constantly and equally at all times, showing absolutely no meaningful variation based on physiologic state or context","Most active during stress, exertion, or emergency; increases cardiovascular/respiratory activity, muscle tone, alertness, and energy mobilization","Most active during sleep only; produces no measurable cardiovascular, respiratory, or muscular effect of any kind whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sympathetic division is most active during stress, exertion, or emergency, producing increased cardiovascular/respiratory activity, increased muscle tone and alertness, and increased mobilization of energy reserves. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is the parasympathetic division most active, and what are its general effects?","choices":["Most active constantly and continuously, functioning nearly identically to the sympathetic division","Most active only during vigorous exercise or periods of intense physical exertion","Most active during stress or emergency; increases alertness, heart rate, and energy mobilization","Most active during rest; causes relaxation, pupillary constriction, digestion, and energy absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"The parasympathetic division is most active during rest, producing relaxation, pupillary constriction, digestion, and energy absorption. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the enteric nervous system, sometimes called \"the brain of the gut\"?","choices":["A subdivision of the sympathetic nervous system that has no independent function whatsoever and cannot act at all without constant, direct input from the spinal cord itself","Neurons located in the walls of the digestive tract that can initiate many visceral reflexes independent of the brain and spinal cord, while receiving input from the ANS","A division of the autonomic nervous system that only innervates the heart and lungs directly, never the digestive tract itself, despite what its common nickname suggests","A division of the central nervous system located entirely within the lower spinal cord segments, controlling only basic spinal reflex arcs and nothing else at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"The enteric nervous system consists of neurons located in the walls of the digestive tract; many visceral reflexes are initiated by the ENS independent of the brain and spinal cord, though it receives input from the ANS. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Where are the sympathetic chains located, and what is the difference between preganglionic and postganglionic fibers?","choices":["Both fiber types are entirely identical in length, myelination status, and the specific neurotransmitter each one releases at its synaptic terminal, with absolutely no meaningful difference recognized between them","Parallel chains on either side of the spinal cord in the thoracolumbar region; preganglionic fibers are short/myelinated (release acetylcholine), postganglionic fibers are long/unmyelinated (mostly release norepinephrine)","Located within the cranial vault only, not the spinal cord; preganglionic fibers are long and unmyelinated, postganglionic fibers are short and myelinated, and both release acetylcholine exclusively at every synapse","Located only within the cranial vault, never alongside the spinal cord itself; both preganglionic and postganglionic fiber types release only acetylcholine at every single synapse they form"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sympathetic chains are parallel chains on either side of the spinal cord in the thoracolumbar region; preganglionic fibers are short and myelinated, extending from the spinal cord to the sympathetic chain (releasing acetylcholine), while postganglionic fibers are long and unmyelinated, extending to effector organs (mostly releasing norepinephrine). — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What do abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves carry, and what are the four named splanchnic nerves?","choices":["Carry only presynaptic fibers directly to the heart and lungs; named as the cardiac, pulmonary, gastric, and hepatic splanchnic nerves","Carry only general sensory fibers originating from the skin of the abdomen and pelvis, never any motor or autonomic fibers at all","Carry postsynaptic fibers to abdominal and pelvic viscera; the greater, lesser, least, and lumbar splanchnic nerves","Carry parasympathetic fibers exclusively, and are instead named the vagal, pelvic, and sacral splanchnic nerve groups"],"answer":2,"exp":"Abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves carry postsynaptic fibers to abdominal and pelvic viscera, and include the greater splanchnic nerve, lesser splanchnic nerve, least splanchnic nerve, and lumbar splanchnic nerve. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What do cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry, and where distal to the paravertebral ganglia do all sympathetic nerves become splanchnic nerves?","choices":["Carry postsynaptic fibers to thoracic viscera; distal to the paravertebral ganglia, all sympathetic nerves become splanchnic nerves","Carry only presynaptic fibers to thoracic viscera, and are said to never carry any postsynaptic fibers whatsoever","Carry postsynaptic fibers exclusively to the lower limbs, entirely bypassing the thoracic visceral organs altogether","Are entirely unrelated to the paravertebral ganglia, arising instead directly from the dorsal spinal nerve roots themselves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry postsynaptic fibers to thoracic viscera, and distal to the paravertebral ganglia, all sympathetic nerves become splanchnic nerves. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves and spinal segments are involved in the parasympathetic division?","choices":["Thoracolumbar spinal segments T1 through L2 only, with absolutely no cranial nerve involvement at all","General visceral efferent fibers of CN III, VII, IX, and X, plus the bulbar portion of CN XI, and sacral segments S2-S4","General somatic afferent fibers of CN V, VI, VIII, and XII, along with cervical spinal segments C1 through C4","Only cranial nerves III and IV, entirely without any spinal cord segment involvement whatsoever"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parasympathetic division involves the general visceral efferent fibers of CN III, VII, IX, and X, and the bulbar portion of CN XI, along with sacral segments S2 to S4. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What type of neurotransmitter do all parasympathetic neurons release, and what receptor do they stimulate at ganglionic synapses?","choices":["Acetylcholine; stimulates only muscarinic receptors at the ganglion, which is always inhibitory","Norepinephrine (adrenergic); stimulates alpha and beta receptors, which is always inhibitory in effect","Dopamine; stimulates nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, which is always an inhibitory effect","Acetylcholine (cholinergic); stimulates nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, which is always excitatory"],"answer":3,"exp":"All parasympathetic neurons are cholinergic, releasing acetylcholine (ACh); at ganglionic synapses, ACh stimulates nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, and this effect is always excitatory. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"At the neuroeffector junction, what receptor does ACh stimulate, and can the effect be excitatory or inhibitory?","choices":["Nicotinic receptors; the effect is always purely excitatory in nature","Muscarinic receptors; the effect can be either excitatory or inhibitory","Nicotinic receptors; the effect is always purely inhibitory in nature","Adrenergic receptors; the effect is always purely excitatory in nature"],"answer":1,"exp":"At neuroeffector junctions, ACh stimulates muscarinic receptors, and the effect can be either excitatory or inhibitory depending on the target tissue. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What makes the cavernous sinuses unique, in terms of their relationship to cranial nerves and arteries?","choices":["The cavernous sinus contains no cranial nerve or arterial relationships at all, being an isolated venous space","No cranial nerves whatsoever are located anywhere near the cavernous sinus along its entire course through the skull","Only CN VI is located in the cavernous sinus, with no arterial relationship present at all in this region","CN III, IV, V1, and V2 are located within the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus; CN VI and the internal carotid artery pass through the center of the sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cavernous sinuses are unique due to their close relationship to multiple cranial nerves and the internal carotid arteries: CN III, IV, V1, and V2 are located within the lateral wall, while CN VI and the internal carotid artery pass through the center of the sinus. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What do paired superior petrosal and transverse sinuses funnel blood into?","choices":["The sigmoid sinuses","The straight sinus","The cavernous sinuses","The superior sagittal sinus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Paired superior petrosal and transverse sinuses funnel blood into the sigmoid sinuses, which are continuous with the internal jugular veins. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are sigmoid sinuses continuous with?","choices":["The vertebral arteries","The internal jugular veins","The internal carotid arteries","The superior vena cava directly, bypassing the jugular veins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Paired sigmoid sinuses are continuous with the internal jugular veins. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"According to the material's summary, where does all venous blood from the cranium eventually lead?","choices":["The hepatic portal vein","The Inferior Vena Cava","The pulmonary vein","The Superior Vena Cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"\"All roads lead to the Superior Vena Cava\" — venous return from the cranium ultimately drains to the superior vena cava. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the straight sinus drain?","choices":["The spinal cord exclusively via the epidural venous plexus","Only the orbit and surrounding eye structures","The internal cerebral veins and great cerebral vein","Only the cavernous sinus itself, exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"The straight sinus drains the internal cerebral veins and the great cerebral vein. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What do the cavernous sinuses drain to, and what do they receive venous blood from?","choices":["Drain into the superior sagittal sinus; receive venous blood exclusively from the cerebellum","Drain directly into the superior vena cava; receive venous blood exclusively from the orbit","Drain into the straight sinus; receive venous blood exclusively from the spinal cord","Drain to the petrosal sinuses; receive venous blood from the orbit as well as the brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cavernous sinuses drain to the petrosal sinuses and receive venous blood from the orbit as well as the brain. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where do left and right venous sinuses converge, and where does this converged point drain to?","choices":["Converge at the transverse sinuses at the confluence of sinuses; this drains to the sigmoid sinus, then to the internal jugular veins","Converge in the subarachnoid space; this drains onward to the choroid plexus for reabsorption","Converge specifically at the cavernous sinus, which then drains directly into the vertebral artery","Converge at the straight sinus only; this single sinus then drains directly onward into the aorta"],"answer":0,"exp":"Venous sinuses converge at the left and right transverse sinuses at the confluence of sinuses, which drains to the sigmoid sinus, and from there to the internal jugular veins, and eventually the superior vena cava. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which artery is described as branching many times in the area of the pons after the vertebral arteries fuse to form it?","choices":["The anterior cerebral artery","The posterior communicating artery","The basilar artery","The internal carotid artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The basilar artery, formed by the fusion of the vertebral arteries, branches many times in the area of the pons. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which two arteries connect the basilar artery and the internal carotid arteries, per the material?","choices":["The superior and inferior cerebellar arteries","The middle cerebral and ophthalmic arteries","Posterior cerebral and posterior communicating arteries","The anterior cerebral and anterior communicating arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior cerebral and posterior communicating arteries connect the basilar artery and the internal carotid arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What structure in a representative neuron diagram represents the initial segment of the axon?","choices":["The region just distal to the axon hillock, where the axon begins before becoming myelinated","The terminal end of the axon located at the synapse itself","The specific region where the dendrites first emerge and branch from the cell body","The nucleus of the neuron, located within its cell body region"],"answer":0,"exp":"The initial segment of the axon is the region just distal to the axon hillock, where the axon begins (before it may become myelinated further along its length). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Are cold receptors or hot receptors more numerous, per the material?","choices":["Hot receptors are more numerous than cold receptors","Cold receptors are more numerous than hot receptors","Cold and hot receptors are exactly equal in number","Neither cold nor hot receptors exist as a distinct category"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cold receptors are more numerous than hot receptors. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are the three types of mechanoreceptors described in the material?","choices":["Only tactile receptors exist as a mechanoreceptor type","Tactile receptors, baroreceptors, and proprioceptors","Photoreceptors, chemoreceptors, and thermoreceptors","Nociceptors, thermoreceptors, and chemoreceptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three types of mechanoreceptors are tactile receptors, baroreceptors, and proprioceptors. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the difference between fine touch/pressure receptors and crude touch/pressure receptors (tactile receptor subtypes)?","choices":["Fine touch receptors monitor internal organ stretch and blood pressure; crude touch receptors monitor only joint angle and tendon tension changes","Fine and crude touch receptors are simply alternate names for baroreceptors that monitor organ stretch due to pressure","Fine touch/pressure receptors provide only generalized information about location; crude touch/pressure receptors provide detailed shape and texture information","Fine touch/pressure receptors provide detailed information about location, shape, size, and texture; crude touch/pressure receptors provide generalized information about stimulus location"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fine touch and pressure receptors provide detailed information about the location, shape, size, and texture of a stimulus, while crude touch and pressure receptors provide generalized information about stimulus location. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What are baroreceptors, and where are they located?","choices":["Receptors that monitor joint position and tension changes, located only in the tendons and joint capsules of the limbs","Stretch receptors that monitor changes in organ stretch due to pressure, located in structures like the stomach, small intestine, urinary bladder, carotid artery, lungs, and large intestine","Receptors that detect light touch and vibration, located only in the epidermis and dermis of the skin","Chemoreceptors that monitor blood chemical composition and oxygen levels, located in the carotid and aortic bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Baroreceptors are stretch receptors that monitor changes in the stretch of organs due to pressure, located in structures like the stomach, small intestine, urinary bladder, carotid artery, lungs, and large intestine. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What do proprioceptors monitor, and what are Golgi tendon organs versus muscle spindles?","choices":["Proprioceptors monitor skin temperature and stretch; Golgi tendon organs monitor muscle fiber length, while muscle spindles monitor tendon tension","Proprioceptors monitor only skin stretch and vibration; Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles are actually the same structure with different names","Proprioceptors monitor blood pressure and organ stretch; Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles are both located exclusively in ligaments","Proprioceptors monitor joint position and tension; Golgi tendon organs are receptors in tendons monitoring tension, while muscle spindles are receptors in muscles monitoring fiber length"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proprioceptors monitor the position of joints and tension in tendons/ligaments; Golgi tendon organs are the receptors in tendons that monitor tension, while muscle spindles are receptors in the muscles that monitor the length of muscle fibers upon contraction. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Where are chemoreceptors found, per the material?","choices":["Only in the retina and cornea, where they respond to changes in ambient light and pH","Only in the skin's epidermis and hair follicles, responding to sweat gland chemical secretions","Respiratory centers of the medulla oblongata, carotid arteries (carotid bodies), and the aortic arch (aortic bodies)","Only in the cerebellum and pons, monitoring cerebrospinal fluid glucose and pH levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chemoreceptors, which detect small changes in the concentration of chemicals and respond to water-soluble or lipid-soluble compounds, are found in the respiratory centers of the medulla oblongata, the carotid arteries (carotid bodies), and the aortic arch (aortic bodies). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What sensations do Merkel discs detect, and which tract do they relay through?","choices":["Light touch; relay through the ventral spinothalamic tract","Pain; relay through Lissauer's tract","Pressure, heat, and skin stretching; relay through the dorsal columns","Deep touch and high-frequency vibration; relay through the dorsal columns"],"answer":0,"exp":"Merkel discs detect light touch and relay this information through the ventral spinothalamic tract. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What sensations do Meissner's corpuscles detect, and which pathway do they relay through?","choices":["Pressure and skin stretching; relay through the ventral spinothalamic tract pathway","Pain and temperature sensation only; relay through the lateral spinothalamic tract","Light touch, movement, and low-frequency vibration; relay through the dorsal columns","Deep touch and high-frequency vibration; relay through the spinothalamic tract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Meissner's corpuscles detect light touch, movement, and low-frequency vibration, relaying this information through the dorsal columns. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What do Ruffini corpuscles detect, and how is their heat sensation relayed differently from their pressure/stretch sensation?","choices":["Detect light touch and movement only; relays exclusively through the ventral spinothalamic tract pathway","Detect deep touch and high-frequency vibration only; all sensations relay exclusively through the dorsal columns","Detect only pain and temperature; all sensations relay exclusively through the spinothalamic tract","Detect pressure, heat, and skin stretching; pressure/stretch relays through the dorsal columns, but heat instead goes to the spinothalamic tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ruffini corpuscles detect pressure, heat, and skin stretching; pressure and stretch sensations relay through the dorsal columns, but heat sensation instead goes to the spinothalamic tract. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What do Pacinian corpuscles detect, and which pathway relays this information?","choices":["Light touch and low-frequency vibration; relay through the spinothalamic tract","Pain; relay through the ventral spinothalamic tract","Pressure and heat; relay through Lissauer's tract","Deep touch and high-frequency vibrations; relay through the dorsal columns"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pacinian corpuscles detect deep touch and high-frequency vibrations, relaying this information through the dorsal columns. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Through which foramen does the facial nerve (CN VII) exit the skull toward the face?","choices":["Through the foramen ovale","Through the optic canal","The stylomastoid foramen","Through the jugular foramen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The facial nerve exits the skull toward the face through the stylomastoid foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the function of the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), and what two nerves does it consist of?","choices":["Motor function (eye movement); consists of a single undivided nerve trunk","A sensory function for taste; composed of the greater and lesser petrosal nerve branches","Sensory function (balance and hearing); consists of the vestibular nerve and the cochlear nerve","A mixed function governing swallowing and salivation; composed of branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) has a sensory function for balance and hearing, originating from receptors of the vestibule and cochlea, and consists of the vestibular nerve (traveling to the vestibular nuclei of the medulla) and the cochlear nerve (synapsing in the cochlear nuclei of the medulla). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the mixed function of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)?","choices":["Sensory function: tongue pain (posterior 1/3 of tongue); motor function: swallowing (via salivary gland/pharyngeal muscles)","Sensory function: hearing loss; motor function: controls facial expression muscles","Sensory function limited to vision loss; motor function limited to controlling eye movement","Sensory function: smell perception only; motor function: none present at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) has a mixed sensory function (pain sensation from the posterior 1/3 of the tongue, originating there) and motor function (swallowing, with visceral motor to the parotid salivary gland and somatic motor to pharyngeal muscles), passing through the jugular foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where are the sensory neurons of CN IX located?","choices":["In the otic ganglion only, with no other sensory ganglion","In the superior and inferior ganglions","In the geniculate ganglion","In the semilunar ganglion"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sensory neurons of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) are located in the superior and inferior ganglions. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the sensory versus motor function of the vagus nerve (CN X)?","choices":["Sensory: information from organs; Motor: sends information to organs (respiratory, cardiovascular, and digestive)","Sensory: light and color vision; Motor: controls extrinsic eye muscle movement and pupil constriction","Sensory: balance and hearing information from the inner ear; Motor: none — this nerve is purely sensory","Sensory: taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue; Motor: controls the muscles of facial expression"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vagus nerve (CN X) has a sensory function (information from the organs, arriving at autonomic centers of the medulla oblongata) and a motor function (sending information to organs — somatic motor to muscles of the palate/pharynx, and visceral motor to the respiratory, cardiovascular, and digestive organs). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Through which foramen does the vagus nerve (CN X) pass, and where are its sensory ganglia located?","choices":["Internal acoustic meatus; sensory neurons in the spiral and vestibular ganglia","Foramen ovale; sensory neurons located in the trigeminal (semilunar) ganglion","Jugular foramen; sensory neurons in the superior and inferior ganglions","Stylomastoid foramen; sensory neurons located in the geniculate ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vagus nerve passes through the jugular foramen, and its sensory neurons are located in the superior and inferior ganglions. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What muscles does the accessory nerve (CN XI) control, and what are its origin and foramen?","choices":["Controls the extra-ocular muscles (superior/inferior/medial rectus, inferior oblique); originates from the mesencephalon, passing through the superior orbital fissure","Controls only the muscles of facial expression; originates from the pons, passing through the internal acoustic meatus and stylomastoid foramen","Controls only the muscles of the tongue (except palatoglossus); originates from the medulla oblongata, passing through the hypoglossal canal","Controls the sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, palate, pharynx, and larynx muscles; originates from the spinal cord and medulla oblongata, passing through the jugular foramen"],"answer":3,"exp":"The accessory nerve (CN XI) controls the sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, palate, pharynx, and larynx muscles, originating from both the spinal cord and medulla oblongata, and passes through the jugular foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the difference between the internal branch and external branch of the accessory nerve (CN XI)?","choices":["Internal branch innervates muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx; external branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Internal branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; external branch innervates the palate, pharynx, and larynx","Internal branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid only; external branch innervates the trapezius, palate, pharynx, and larynx","Internal branch is purely sensory to the pharynx and larynx; external branch is purely motor to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius"],"answer":0,"exp":"The internal branch of CN XI innervates the muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx, while the external branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the function of the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), and through what foramen does it pass?","choices":["Sensory function, providing taste from the tongue; passes through the jugular foramen","Motor function, controlling facial expression; passes through the stylomastoid foramen","Mixed sensory/motor function for hearing and balance; passes through the internal acoustic meatus","Motor function, controlling tongue movement; passes through the hypoglossal canal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) has a motor function, controlling tongue movement, originating from the medulla oblongata and passing through the hypoglossal canal to reach the tongue muscles. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve passes through the optic canal?","choices":["Optic nerve (CN II)","Trigeminal nerve, ophthalmic division (CN V1)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The optic nerve (CN II) passes through the optic canal. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
